<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Apple privacy manifest for the Runner target.

	This file describes what the *app's own code* collects and which
	required-reason APIs it touches. Apple aggregates it with the manifest
	each dynamically linked pod ships (the Podfile uses `use_frameworks!`,
	so Sentry's and PostHog's own manifests are processed automatically)
	into the privacy report Xcode generates at archive time.

	Two rules govern every edit below:

	  1. It describes the *shipped binary*, not a local debug run. Sentry and
	     PostHog are opt-in at build time (SentryConstants.isEnabled /
	     PosthogConstants.isEnabled), so a build with no --dart-define
	     collects none of the diagnostics or usage data declared here. The
	     release builds that go to App Store Connect do supply those defines,
	     and one manifest ships for every configuration, so the declarations
	     describe the enabled case. Under-declaring is what gets a build
	     rejected (ITMS-91053); declaring a type a given build happens not to
	     exercise is not.

	  2. `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked` is `true` wherever it is, because
	     signing in sets a Sentry user and a PostHog person to the backend's
	     user id (see AuthState._applyIdentity in auth_provider.dart). Only
	     the id is ever sent — never email or name, which the redaction list
	     strips — but an id that identifies an account is what Apple means by
	     "linked to the user". Anonymous use is unlinked; the manifest has no
	     way to say "sometimes", so it states the broader case.

	Keep this in sync with the App Store Connect privacy questionnaire and
	with android/app/src/main/.../data-safety notes: the manifest and the
	store answers are checked against each other, and a mismatch is a review
	finding even when each is individually defensible.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No ad SDK, no IDFA/IDFV read, no ATT prompt, and nothing is shared
	     with a data broker. PostHog's anonymous distinct_id is an
	     install-scoped UUID it generates itself, not a device identifier, and
	     `personProfiles = identifiedOnly` keeps anonymous use anonymous. If
	     an attribution or ad SDK is ever added, this flips to <true/> and the
	     app must present the ATT prompt. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Must stay empty while NSPrivacyTracking is false: a non-empty list
	     here with tracking off is rejected as contradictory. Sentry's and
	     PostHog's ingest hosts are not tracking domains — they receive the
	     app's own diagnostics, not cross-app activity. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Better Auth sign-up/sign-in posts an email address to the app's
		     own backend (AuthRepository.signIn / signUp). Collected by the
		     app even though it never reaches Sentry or PostHog — Apple counts
		     transmission to your own server as collection. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- AuthRepository.signUp sends a display name. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- The backend's user id, used as the correlation key in Sentry
		     (SentryUser(id:)) and as the PostHog distinct id once identified. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Sentry crash reports. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Release health. `enableAutoSessionTracking = true` sends a session
		     ping per launch, which is what produces the crash-free rate.
		     Performance *tracing* is off (`tracesSampleRate = 0.0`); if that
		     stays off forever this entry could go, but it must come back the
		     moment the sample rate is raised. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Sentry's device/OS context and lifecycle breadcrumbs. Screenshots
		     and view hierarchies are deliberately off, and no Dio integration
		     is installed, so no request URL, header, or body is attached. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDiagnosticData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- PostHog screen views and events, plus feature-flag evaluation.
		     Session replay and surveys are both off by configuration, so no
		     screen content is captured.

		     These two mirror what posthog_flutter's own bundled manifest
		     declares, with one deliberate difference: it marks them
		     `Linked = false`, because an SDK cannot know whether the host app
		     calls `identify()`. This app does, so the app-level declaration
		     has to say `true`. Where an SDK manifest and this file disagree,
		     this file is the one describing reality. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUsageData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- Required-reason APIs. Each reason code is drawn from Apple's closed
	     list for its category; an unlisted code fails validation just as a
	     missing declaration does. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- CA92.1 — "read and write data exclusive to the app itself".
		     shared_preferences backs the theme mode, the onboarding and tour
		     flags, and the `analytics-identified` marker. This is the single
		     most common cause of ITMS-91053.

		     Not redundant with the plugin: shared_preferences_foundation's
		     own manifest declares 1C8F.1 (the App Group case), which does not
		     cover this app's use of defaults that are exclusively its own. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- C617.1 and 35F9.1 cover Sentry's native SDK: it stamps cached
		     envelopes with file timestamps and measures app-start against
		     system boot time, and per Sentry's own guidance neither can be
		     opted out of.

		     Worth knowing where these actually come from. The `sentry_flutter`
		     pub package ships no manifest of its own; the manifest lives in
		     the Sentry/HybridSDK pod it depends on (sentry-cocoa 8.58.4),
		     which declares exactly these two plus UserDefaults CA92.1. Apple
		     aggregates that automatically while the pod is dynamically linked
		     (`use_frameworks!` in the Podfile), so the entries here are
		     redundant today — and load-bearing the moment someone switches
		     the Podfile to `:linkage => :static`, which is precisely the
		     change nobody remembers to re-audit. Bump `Sentry/HybridSDK` and
		     re-check: these codes track the pod, not this file. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
